#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700570 :question/problem "Given $f(x)=x^{10}$ and $F'(x)=f(x)$, a student proposes\n\n$$\nG(x)=\\frac{x^{11}}{11}+C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the proposed expression, then give the correct general antiderivative of $f$. Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant.\n\n1. $G'(x)=$ {{derivative}}.\n2. $F(x)=$ {{antiderivative}}." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the proposed expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{11}}{11}+C\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{11}\\cdot 11x^{10}+0 \\\\ &=x^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe proposed expression does differentiate to the given function.\n\nIntegration reverses differentiation, so $F'(x)=f(x)$ gives $F(x)=\\int f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nApply the power rule with $n=10$ and include an arbitrary constant $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x)&=\\int x^{10}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{10+1}}{10+1}+C \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{11}}{11}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{11}}{11}+C\\right)&=\\frac{1}{11}\\cdot 11x^{10}+0 \\\\ &=x^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700573 :question/problem "Given $f(x)=x^{4}$ and $F'(x)=f(x)$, a student proposes\n\n$$\nG(x)=\\frac{x^{5}}{4}+C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the proposed expression, then give the correct general antiderivative of $f$. Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant.\n\n1. $G'(x)=$ {{derivative}}.\n2. $F(x)=$ {{antiderivative}}." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the proposed expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{4}+C\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 5x^{4}+0 \\\\ &=\\frac{5 x^{4}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe proposed expression divides by the old exponent rather than the new exponent.\n\nIntegration reverses differentiation, so $F'(x)=f(x)$ gives $F(x)=\\int f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nApply the power rule with $n=4$ and include an arbitrary constant $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x)&=\\int x^{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{4+1}}{4+1}+C \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{5}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{5}}{5}+C\\right)&=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 5x^{4}+0 \\\\ &=x^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700582 :question/problem "Given $f(x)=x^{7}$ and $F'(x)=f(x)$, a student proposes\n\n$$\nG(x)=8 x^{8}+C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the proposed expression, then give the correct general antiderivative of $f$. Use $C$ for the arbitrary constant.\n\n1. $G'(x)=$ {{derivative}}.\n2. $F(x)=$ {{antiderivative}}." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate the proposed expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}G'(x)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(8 x^{8}+C\\right) \\\\ &=8\\cdot 8x^{7}+0 \\\\ &=64 x^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe proposed expression multiplies by the new exponent rather than dividing.\n\nIntegration reverses differentiation, so $F'(x)=f(x)$ gives $F(x)=\\int f(x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nApply the power rule with $n=7$ and include an arbitrary constant $C$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x)&=\\int x^{7}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{7+1}}{7+1}+C \\\\ &=\\frac{x^{8}}{8}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCheck by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{x^{8}}{8}+C\\right)&=\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 8x^{7}+0 \\\\ &=x^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700658 :question/problem "Complete the table for $y=1-\\dfrac{x}{2}$. Then plot the ordered pairs on paper and select the graph of the line.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | {{value-1}} | {{value-2}} | {{value-3}} | {{value-4}} |\n\nThe axes in every graph use one unit per grid square." :question/worked-solution "Substitute each given value of $x$ into $y=1-\\dfrac{x}{2}$ to find its corresponding $y$-value.\n\n- If $x=-4$, then $y=1-\\dfrac{-4}{2}=1+2=3$.\n\n- If $x=-2$, then $y=1-\\dfrac{-2}{2}=1+1=2$.\n\n- If $x=2$, then $y=1-\\dfrac{2}{2}=1-1=0$.\n\n- If $x=4$, then $y=1-\\dfrac{4}{2}=1-2=-1$.\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ | $-1$ |\n\nPlot the ordered pairs $(-4,3)$, $(-2,2)$, $(2,0)$, $(4,-1)$:\n\n![](images/17/175b18e01817f344aa2290a60e6dabc96f96e1eecf53c510c9062d6f4912cb87.png)\n\nDraw a straight line through the points and extend it in both directions. This is the graph of $y=1-\\dfrac{x}{2}$:\n\n![](images/6e/6e50f8b3df565d1f92e0646e698df34cd1a64e8670b2f2529ef76264992d22ce.png)\n\nKeep the coordinates in $(x,y)$ order. For example, the point for $x=-4$ is $(-4,3)$, not $(3,-4)$. The line passes through all four calculated points; matching its downward direction alone is insufficient."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700661 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=x+1$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Substitute each given value of $x$ into $y=x+1$ to find its corresponding $y$-value.\n\n- If $x=0$, then $y=0+1=1$.\n\n- If $x=1$, then $y=1+1=2$.\n\n- If $x=2$, then $y=2+1=3$.\n\n- If $x=3$, then $y=3+1=4$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nPlot the ordered pairs $(0,1)$, $(1,2)$, $(2,3)$, $(3,4)$:\n\n![](images/0d/0def264a2f1048823772687bebba3e983773c325a1d3a819fa17eefc8aae65a0.png)\n\nDraw a straight line through the points and extend it in both directions. This is the graph of $y=x+1$:\n\n![](images/31/3197955a6343384cc573249dca120c5e635c2f4ebb87f2216409819f39285900.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700676 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=2-2x$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "Substitute each given value of $x$ into $y=2-2x$ to find its corresponding $y$-value.\n\n- If $x=0$, then $y=2-2(0)=2$.\n\n- If $x=1$, then $y=2-2(1)=0$.\n\n- If $x=2$, then $y=2-2(2)=-2$.\n\n- If $x=3$, then $y=2-2(3)=-4$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $2$ | $0$ | $-2$ | $-4$ |\n\nPlot the ordered pairs $(0,2)$, $(1,0)$, $(2,-2)$, $(3,-4)$:\n\n![](images/11/115f577ba0aab6d656adc1dc790f75f52c630c4ab7f7db225d0e6873b2b911bc.png)\n\nDraw a straight line through the points and extend it in both directions. This is the graph of $y=2-2x$:\n\n![](images/b8/b8e4984a956e952fa4f356920899c3a6c86839ff6af849aebe375d67fda3957b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700688 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C 3yz\\cos x\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,\\sin t,1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le\\frac\\pi2.\n$$\n\nGive the exact value." :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar line integral formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve, $x=t$, $y=\\sin t$, and $z=1$. Hence\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=3\\sin t\\cos t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate all three coordinates and compute the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 1,\\cos t,0\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1+\\cos^2t}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\int_C 3yz\\cos x\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}3\\sin t\\cos t\\sqrt{1+\\cos^2t}\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nChoose $u=1+\\cos^2t$. Then $\\mathrm du=-2\\sin t\\cos t\\,\\mathrm dt$. The limits reverse:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac\\pi2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $1$ |\n\nSo\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C 3yz\\cos x\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=-\\frac32\\int_2^1 u^{1/2}\\,\\mathrm du\\\\\n&=\\frac32\\int_1^2 u^{1/2}\\,\\mathrm du\\\\\n&=\\left[u^{3/2}\\right]_1^2\\\\\n&=2\\sqrt2-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integrand is nonnegative on this interval, which also checks the sign."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700698 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t,\\frac{t^2}{2},t^2\\right\\rangle,\\qquad 1\\le t\\le\\sqrt3.\n$$\n\nGive the exact value." :question/worked-solution "For a scalar function in three variables,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nHere $x=t$, $y=t^2/2$, and $z=t^2$. Since $t\\ge1$, the denominator is nonzero, and\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=\\frac{t^2/2+t^2}{t}=\\frac32t.\n$$\n\nAll three derivative components contribute to arc length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 1,t,2t\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{1^2+t^2+(2t)^2}=\\sqrt{1+5t^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\mathrm ds\n=\\int_1^{\\sqrt3}\\frac32t\\sqrt{1+5t^2}\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nChoose $u=1+5t^2$, so $\\mathrm du=10t\\,\\mathrm dt$ and $\\frac32t\\,\\mathrm dt=\\frac3{20}\\,\\mathrm du$. The transformed limits are\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $6$ | $16$ |\n\nIt follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C\\frac{y+z}{x}\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\frac3{20}\\int_6^{16}u^{1/2}\\,\\mathrm du\\\\\n&=\\frac1{10}\\left[u^{3/2}\\right]_6^{16}\\\\\n&=\\frac{64-6\\sqrt6}{10}\\\\\n&=\\frac{32-3\\sqrt6}{5}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700700 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of $f(x,y)=6y$ along the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t^2,t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nUse\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nFor this curve, $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{4t^2+1}$. Use the substitution $u=4t^2+1$ and give the exact value." :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, $x=t^2$ and $y=t$, so $f(\\mathbf r(t))=6t$. The supplied speed follows from\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t)=\\langle 2t,1\\rangle,\\qquad\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|=\\sqrt{(2t)^2+1^2}=\\sqrt{4t^2+1}.\n$$\n\nThus\n\n$$\n\\int_C 6y\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_0^1 6t\\sqrt{4t^2+1}\\,\\mathrm dt.\n$$\n\nWith $u=4t^2+1$, we have $\\mathrm du=8t\\,\\mathrm dt$ and $6t\\,\\mathrm dt=\\frac34\\,\\mathrm du$. Change both limits:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $5$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C 6y\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\frac34\\int_1^5 u^{1/2}\\,\\mathrm du\\\\\n&=\\frac12\\left[u^{3/2}\\right]_1^5\\\\\n&=\\frac{5\\sqrt5-1}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700718 :question/problem "Use two points to sketch $y=\\frac{1}{2}x-2$.\n\nComplete the two $y$-coordinates, then choose the graph through both points.\n\nWhen $x=0$, $y=$ {{y-zero}}.\n\nWhen $x=4$, $y=$ {{y-four}}." :question/worked-solution "Substitute each given $x$-coordinate into the equation.\n\nFor $x=0$,\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}(0)-2=-2,\n$$\nso the first point is $(0,-2)$.\n\nFor $x=4$,\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}(4)-2=2-2=0,\n$$\nso the second point is $(4,0)$.\n\nPlot the two points and draw the straight line through them. Several choices pass through $(0,-2)$; the required graph also passes through $(4,0)$.\n\n![](images/1d/1dd329828659b6c264ed7beab92b582306beacc3a83da4a14bafabd57f6c7852.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700720 :question/problem "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-x+1$?" :question/worked-solution "Find two distinct points by substituting convenient values of $x$.\n\nFor $x=0$,\n$$\ny=-(0)+1=1,\n$$\nso one point is $(0,1)$.\n\nFor $x=2$,\n$$\ny=-(2)+1=-1,\n$$\nso another point is $(2,-1)$.\n\nPlot $(0,1)$ and $(2,-1)$ and draw a straight line through them. Choose the graph containing both points. A line through just one of them is not enough.\n\n![](images/d4/d4663aff93056fa7baadc80f556a78766dcb4ffe3f1aba9adba1a18833abb76b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700728 :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-2$ and $x=6$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-2$: {{point-left}}\n\nPoint with $x=6$: {{point-right}}" :question/worked-solution "Find the corresponding $y$-coordinates.\n\nFor $x=-2$,\n$$\ny=2-\\frac{1}{2}(-2)=2+1=3,\n$$\nso the point is $(-2,3)$.\n\nFor $x=6$,\n$$\ny=2-\\frac{1}{2}(6)=2-3=-1,\n$$\nso the point is $(6,-1)$.\n\nPlot these two distinct points and draw a straight line through them. All five choices pass through $(0,2)$, so the $y$-intercept alone cannot distinguish them. The required graph passes through both $(-2,3)$ and $(6,-1)$.\n\n![](images/a4/a407efda95c6f357462e5dafbcd38683533656a2d84456b1414a31dd1182af30.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700866 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C (xy+z)\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t^2+1,t^2-1,t^2\\rangle$ for $-2\\le t\\le-1$?" :question/worked-solution "Along the curve, $x=t^2+1$, $y=t^2-1$, and $z=t^2$. Compute the derivative and speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle2t,2t,2t\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(2t)^2+(2t)^2+(2t)^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{12t^2}=2\\sqrt{3}\\,|t|.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery $t$ in $[-2,-1]$ is negative, so $|t|=-t$. Therefore the speed is $-2\\sqrt{3}\\,t$, which is positive on this interval.\n\nSubstitute into the scalar function, keeping the product grouped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=(t^2+1)(t^2-1)+t^2\\\\\n&=t^4-1+t^2\\\\\n&=t^4+t^2-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the scalar line-integral formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nCombining these expressions, with the original parameter bounds, gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C (xy+z)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_{-2}^{-1}(t^4+t^2-1)(-2\\sqrt{3}\\,t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=-2\\sqrt{3}\\int_{-2}^{-1}t(t^4+t^2-1)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing $2\\sqrt{3}\\,t$ as the speed would give a negative speed and the wrong sign for the integral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700869 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C (2x-y^2-z^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t^2,t-2,t+2\\rangle$ for $0\\le t\\le1$?" :question/worked-solution "Here, $x=t^2$, $y=t-2$, and $z=t+2$.\n\nFirst compute the derivative and speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle2t,1,1\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(2t)^2+1^2+1^2}=\\sqrt{4t^2+2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute all three coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=2t^2-(t-2)^2-(t+2)^2\\\\\n&=2t^2-(t^2-4t+4)-(t^2+4t+4)\\\\\n&=-8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth squares are subtracted, including their constant terms. The scalar function is negative on the whole path, even though speed is nonnegative.\n\nUse the scalar line-integral formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nCombining the substituted function with the speed gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C (2x-y^2-z^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-8\\int_0^1\\sqrt{4t^2+2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700872 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\cos t,\\sin t\\rangle$ for $0\\le t\\le\\pi/2$?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f5/f53d78debfec5f8c50e0f3a5947d2a36126e18c073d7b0cc4c59d506753059b3.png)\n\nThe path is the quarter circle shown above. Along the curve, $x=\\cos t$ and $y=\\sin t$, so\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=\\cos t\\,(\\sin t)^2=\\sin^2 t\\cos t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle-\\sin t,\\cos t\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(-\\sin t)^2+\\cos^2 t}=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the scalar line-integral formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThus the equivalent definite integral is\n\n$$\n\\int_C xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin^2 t\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe speed is $1$; no additional factor is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700880 :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=x-2$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-1$ and $x=1$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-1$: {{point-first}}\n\nPoint with $x=1$: {{point-second}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-2 \\\\  & =(-1)-2 \\\\  & =-1-2 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(-1,-3).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =x-2 \\\\  & =(1)-2 \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=x-2.$\n\n![](images/f5/f585c9c05b54be95aca55b281422086eaf7c868bb53dc7f1949ebd2a56486ecf.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700884 :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=-1+x$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-1$ and $x=1$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-1$: {{point-first}}\n\nPoint with $x=1$: {{point-second}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1+x \\\\  & =-1+(-1) \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(-1,-2).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-1+x \\\\  & =-1+(1) \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-1+x.$\n\n![](images/e2/e2b6910a0221e4c851f7af033e51e9e762eb9e704f15ecb4381a452d1b573af5.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700885 :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-2$ and $x=2$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-2$: {{point-first}}\n\nPoint with $x=2$: {{point-second}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-2.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2}(-2) \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(-2,3).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=2.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2-\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\  & =2-\\frac{1}{2}(2) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=2-\\frac{1}{2}x.$\n\n![](images/d4/d4ba35ffbdc7e88a510be218444bfcf752b94f3569c646ddb75b832f1edb1686.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700918 :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=-2x-2$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-1$ and $x=2$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-1$: {{point-first}}\n\nPoint with $x=2$: {{point-second}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x-2 \\\\  & =-2(-1)-2 \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(-1,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=2.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x-2 \\\\  & =-2(2)-2 \\\\  & =-4-2 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(2,-6).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-2x-2.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1b89e25d6730d1fa3d19af164d8506e8fd115c92515f8f5ea7651f6f5c51b84.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700930 :question/problem "Which graph shows the line $y=- \\frac{1}{2}x-2$?\n\nFirst give the two points on this line with $x=-4$ and $x=2$. Enter each answer as an ordered pair $(x,y)$, then select the graph.\n\nPoint with $x=-4$: {{point-first}}\n\nPoint with $x=2$: {{point-second}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=-4.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =- \\frac{1}{2}x-2 \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{2}(-4)-2 \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(-4,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=2.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =- \\frac{1}{2}x-2 \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{2}(2)-2 \\\\  & =-1-2 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(2,-3).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=- \\frac{1}{2}x-2.$\n\n![](images/fd/fda9429962cb359eb8c9e70137378e1e72273b65d7a968361f6dbd060db07c00.png)\n\nAll choices have the same $y$-intercept. Check another point to distinguish the lines."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700937 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=-2x-2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline x & -3 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\hline y &  &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nEnter the four table values, then select the graph.\n\nFor $x=-3$, enter $y$: {{value-1}}.\n\nFor $x=-1$, enter $y$: {{value-2}}.\n\nFor $x=1$, enter $y$: {{value-3}}.\n\nFor $x=3$, enter $y$: {{value-4}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=-2x-2.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=-3$ then $y=-2(-3)-2=4,$\n\n- if $x=-1$ then $y=-2(-1)-2=0,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=-2(1)-2=-4,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=-2(3)-2=-8.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline x & -3 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\hline y & 4 & 0 & -4 & -8 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/07/076dd57436803bb3d318897e81216dd59bd0c5450108dc73405898ad94da7dce.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=-2x-2.$\n\n![](images/fc/fc293f130f98bed98519e0e90132aa1eb59edf36ca3c76d265cb4167318f6ea9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700938 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=-x-5.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline x & -3 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\hline y &  &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nEnter the four table values, then select the graph.\n\nFor $x=-3$, enter $y$: {{value-1}}.\n\nFor $x=-1$, enter $y$: {{value-2}}.\n\nFor $x=1$, enter $y$: {{value-3}}.\n\nFor $x=3$, enter $y$: {{value-4}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=-x-5.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=-3$ then $y=-(-3)-5=-2,$\n\n- if $x=-1$ then $y=-(-1)-5=-4,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=-(1)-5=-6,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=-(3)-5=-8.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline x & -3 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\hline y & -2 & -4 & -6 & -8 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/c7/c70b1a457d3c0700d6b73040428ff3db18e954429533595cbbf679953d5449b0.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=-x-5.$\n\n![](images/c4/c42f7a1a9ed9ee8779e145ed550139a3a25590fe3c3ffb042a3feab18b782075.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700966 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=2x+2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline x & -3 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\hline y &  &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nEnter the four table values, then select the graph.\n\nFor $x=-3$, enter $y$: {{value-1}}.\n\nFor $x=-1$, enter $y$: {{value-2}}.\n\nFor $x=1$, enter $y$: {{value-3}}.\n\nFor $x=3$, enter $y$: {{value-4}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=2x+2.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=-3$ then $y=2(-3)+2=-4,$\n\n- if $x=-1$ then $y=2(-1)+2=0,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=2(1)+2=4,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=2(3)+2=8.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline x & -3 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\hline y & -4 & 0 & 4 & 8 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/ae/aea0752d4e507c00063c2c5a8ede3a8cbe383de78ede9fc9a17a7218903477f9.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=2x+2.$\n\n![](images/84/840b1a33143718d3fdba042b36cbb29cc5864511cc4a5200c3fcc15821d72705.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700979 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=-x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline x & -3 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\hline y &  &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nEnter the four table values, then select the graph.\n\nFor $x=-3$, enter $y$: {{value-1}}.\n\nFor $x=-1$, enter $y$: {{value-2}}.\n\nFor $x=1$, enter $y$: {{value-3}}.\n\nFor $x=3$, enter $y$: {{value-4}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=-x.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=-3$ then $y=-(-3)=3,$\n\n- if $x=-1$ then $y=-(-1)=1,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=-(1)=-1,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=-(3)=-3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline x & -3 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\hline y & 3 & 1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/4c/4cba48e0ad715435a79ddeae81a38af2bfeba4d2f3ce2f57dbfa157ddebcfe5d.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=-x.$\n\n![](images/b0/b01e5fd4b21edf3181d50d26e2929b481ec9cd93b8585ea475347483ce269655.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186700997 :question/problem "Copy and complete the table below and use it to determine which of the following is the graph of $y=-x+3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline x & -3 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\hline y &  &  &  &  \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nEnter the four table values, then select the graph.\n\nFor $x=-3$, enter $y$: {{value-1}}.\n\nFor $x=-1$, enter $y$: {{value-2}}.\n\nFor $x=1$, enter $y$: {{value-3}}.\n\nFor $x=3$, enter $y$: {{value-4}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we fill the table. Given a value of $x,$ we can find the corresponding $y$ by substituting $x$ into the formula $y=-x+3.$ Therefore,\n\n- if $x=-3$ then $y=-(-3)+3=6,$\n\n- if $x=-1$ then $y=-(-1)+3=4,$\n\n- if $x=1$ then $y=-(1)+3=2,$ and\n\n- if $x=3$ then $y=-(3)+3=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|}\\hline x & -3 & -1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ \\hline y & 6 & 4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ \\hline\\end{array}\n$$\n\nWe plot these $(x,y)$ pairs on the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/02/0221bc27b32d7a33b235c02cae9548973acaa6b8a6838d86422e52629c705713.png)\n\nFinally, connecting the points gives us a straight line. This is the graph of $y=-x+3.$\n\n![](images/90/904dcf2cc1a58be2643dfaf5b00fa7d1ca0835a947d023082100b833537ad977.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701016 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2\\cos t,2\\sin t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a scalar line integral with respect to arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe path is the quarter circle of radius $2$ shown below.\n\n![Quarter circle of radius 2 from (2,0) to (0,2), traversed counterclockwise.](images/6d/6d933c280ebb530aefaadc1b5ef927e39603635960348255045c138ee9bab366.png)\n\nAlong the curve, $x=2\\cos t$ and $y=2\\sin t$. Compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle -2\\sin t,2\\cos t\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{4\\sin^2t+4\\cos^2t}=2,\\\\\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=(2\\sin t)^2=4\\sin^2t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe integral involves $\\sin^2t$, so use the identity $\\sin^2t=(1-\\cos 2t)/2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=8\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\sin^2t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=4\\int_0^{\\pi/2}(1-\\cos 2t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=4\\left[t-\\frac{\\sin 2t}{2}\\right]_0^{\\pi/2}\\\\\n&=4\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-0\\right)=2\\pi.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701025 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n\\int_C (x^2+y^2-2)\\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2(\\cos t+t\\sin t),2(\\sin t-t\\cos t)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le t\\le 1$." :question/worked-solution "For a scalar line integral with respect to arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nUse the product rule in both components. The terms without a factor of $t$ cancel:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx'(t)&=2(-\\sin t+\\sin t+t\\cos t)=2t\\cos t,\\\\\ny'(t)&=2(\\cos t-\\cos t+t\\sin t)=2t\\sin t,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{4t^2\\cos^2t+4t^2\\sin^2t}\\\\\n&=2|t|=2t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $|t|=t$ because $0\\le t\\le 1$. The speed is zero only at the starting endpoint.\n\nNext, substitute both coordinates. Expanding the squares cancels the mixed terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t)^2+y(t)^2\n&=4\\bigl[(\\cos t+t\\sin t)^2+(\\sin t-t\\cos t)^2\\bigr]\\\\\n&=4\\bigl[\\cos^2t+2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\sin^2t\\\\\n&\\qquad+\\sin^2t-2t\\sin t\\cos t+t^2\\cos^2t\\bigr]\\\\\n&=4(1+t^2).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $f(\\mathbf r(t))=4(1+t^2)-2=2+4t^2$. Multiply this by the variable speed and integrate each term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C (x^2+y^2-2)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_0^1 (2+4t^2)\\,2t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1 (4t+8t^3)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[2t^2+2t^4\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=2+2=4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701032 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of $f(x,y)=x+y$ along the curve $C$ parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2t,1+2t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a scalar line integral with respect to arc length, use\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve, $x=2t$ and $y=1+2t$. Differentiate the path and compute its speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle 2,2\\rangle,\\\\\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{2^2+2^2}=2\\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=2t+(1+2t)=1+4t.\n$$\n\nMultiply by the speed before integrating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s\n&=\\int_0^1 (1+4t)\\,2\\sqrt{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=2\\sqrt{2}\\left[t+2t^2\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=2\\sqrt{2}(1+2)=6\\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701041 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf F(x,y)=\\langle xy+y^2,\\,2x^2+e^y\\rangle$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=x^2$ and $y=2x$, traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve. The component functions have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, so Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nThe enclosed region $D$ and its counterclockwise boundary are shown below.\n\n![](images/5f/5f34ac74599937e0ae181126ff9a3da5b29dfe213614a1f23ca10c7062f22f7d.png)\n\nWriting the vector line integral in differential form gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r=\\oint_C(xy+y^2)\\,dx+(2x^2+e^y)\\,dy.\n$$\n\nHere $P=xy+y^2$ and $Q=2x^2+e^y$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=x+2y,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=4x,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=3x-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersections satisfy $x^2=2x$, so $x(x-2)=0$ and $x=0,2$. On $0\\le x\\le2$, the parabola $y=x^2$ is below the line $y=2x$. Thus $D=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le2,\\ x^2\\le y\\le2x\\}$, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r\n&=\\int_0^2\\int_{x^2}^{2x}(3x-2y)\\,dy\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_0^2[3xy-y^2]_{x^2}^{2x}\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_0^2\\bigl((6x^2-4x^2)-(3x^3-x^4)\\bigr)\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_0^2(x^4-3x^3+2x^2)\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{x^5}{5}-\\frac{3x^4}{4}+\\frac{2x^3}{3}\\right]_0^2\\\\\n&=\\frac{32}{5}-12+\\frac{16}{3}=-\\frac{4}{15}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701052 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\oint_C xy\\,dx+(x^2+y^2)\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(1,2)$ and $(0,2)$, traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve. The component functions have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, so Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nThe enclosed region $D$ and its counterclockwise boundary are shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a87f696b1eb42588ab69800b72d821bf47b35676fc19dcd4fe2927bbf36ba3f8.png)\n\nHere $P=xy$ and $Q=x^2+y^2$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2x,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe sloping side through $(0,0)$ and $(1,2)$ is $y=2x$. Vertical slices run from $y=2x$ to $y=2$, with $0\\le x\\le1$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C xy\\,dx+(x^2+y^2)\\,dy\n&=\\int_0^1\\int_{2x}^{2}x\\,dy\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1[xy]_{2x}^{2}\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1(2x-2x^2)\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\left[x^2-\\frac{2x^3}{3}\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=1-\\frac23=\\frac13.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701053 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\oint_C (2y-x)\\,dx+(5x+y)\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the boundary of the rectangle $-1\\le x\\le3$, $0\\le y\\le2$, traversed counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve. The component functions have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, so Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nThe enclosed region $D$ and its counterclockwise boundary are shown below.\n\n![](images/39/3954846460076fa1ef1e5826d32bde9145e7350cb2324072b001d8c6ff41cc14.png)\n\nHere $P=2y-x$ and $Q=5x+y$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=2,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=5,\\\\\nQ_x-P_y&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bounds of $D$ are $-1\\le x\\le3$ and $0\\le y\\le2$. Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C (2y-x)\\,dx+(5x+y)\\,dy\n&=\\int_{-1}^{3}\\int_0^2 3\\,dy\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{3}[3y]_0^2\\,dx\\\\\n&=\\int_{-1}^{3}6\\,dx\\\\\n&=[6x]_{-1}^{3}\\\\\n&=18-(-6)\\\\\n&=24.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, the constant curl $3$ is multiplied by the rectangle's area $(3-(-1))(2-0)=8$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701062 :question/problem "The curve $C$ follows the straight sides of a square in the order\n\n$$\n(0,0)\\longrightarrow(0,1)\\longrightarrow(1,1)\\longrightarrow(1,0)\\longrightarrow(0,0).\n$$\n\nLet $D$ be the interior of the square. Which function $f(x,y)$ is obtained by applying Green's theorem with this traversal in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\oint_C\\left(ye^{xy}+y^2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n+\\left(xe^{xy}+x^2y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\\\\\n&\\qquad=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The ordered vertices trace the left side upward, the top side to the right, the right side downward and the bottom side to the left. The interior stays on the traveler's right, so $C$ is clockwise.\n\n![The shaded unit square with arrows pointing clockwise.](images/91/91aadfb7b45c720c35a0aca6158d87f5e5eae700f9537f259015236df3359c7e.png)\n\nReversing a curve reverses the sign of its line integral. Thus the clockwise version of Green's theorem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n&=-\\iint_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A\\\\\n&=\\iint_D(P_y-Q_x)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $P=ye^{xy}+y^2$ and $Q=xe^{xy}+x^2y$. Both functions have continuous first partial derivatives throughout the plane. Apply the product and chain rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=e^{xy}+xy e^{xy}+2y,\\\\\nQ_x&=e^{xy}+xy e^{xy}+2xy.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow use the order of subtraction appropriate to this clockwise traversal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=P_y-Q_x\\\\\n&=\\left(e^{xy}+xy e^{xy}+2y\\right)\n-\\left(e^{xy}+xy e^{xy}+2xy\\right)\\\\\n&=2y-2xy\\\\\n&=2y(1-x).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBoth exponential terms cancel. Using $Q_x-P_y$ without correcting the orientation would instead give $2xy-2y$, the negative of the required integrand."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701066 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the rectangle with vertices $(0,0)$, $(2,0)$, $(2,1)$ and $(0,1)$, traversed counterclockwise, and let $D$ be its interior. Use Green's theorem to choose the integrand $f(x,y)$ in\n\n$$\n\\oint_C y^2e^{xy}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(2xy e^{xy}+x^2y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a counterclockwise, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve $C$ bounding $D$, Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nprovided $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$.\n\nThe rectangle and its counterclockwise boundary are shown below.\n\n![](images/8d/8d57fb7f6b8e2e822a11f11a0267994c449c37b43df167a8d48ab8036854b8dd.png)\n\nIdentify $P=y^2e^{xy}$ and $Q=2xy e^{xy}+x^2y$. Both required partial derivatives involve a product rule. The chain rule gives $\\partial_y e^{xy}=xe^{xy}$ and $\\partial_x e^{xy}=ye^{xy}$, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_y&=2y e^{xy}+xy^2e^{xy},\\\\\nQ_x&=2y e^{xy}+2xy^2e^{xy}+2xy.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtract the entire derivative $P_y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=Q_x-P_y\\\\\n&=\\left(2y e^{xy}+2xy^2e^{xy}+2xy\\right)\n-\\left(2y e^{xy}+xy^2e^{xy}\\right)\\\\\n&=xy^2e^{xy}+2xy.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2y e^{xy}$ terms cancel, but only one of the two $xy^2e^{xy}$ terms cancels. Hence the line integral equals $\\iint_D\\left(xy^2e^{xy}+2xy\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701069 :question/problem "Let $C$ be the boundary of the square with vertices $(0,0)$, $(1,0)$, $(1,1)$ and $(0,1)$, traversed counterclockwise, and let $D$ be its interior. According to Green's theorem,\n\n$$\n\\oint_C y^2\\,\\mathrm{d}x+(xy+x^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich function $f(x,y)$ is obtained by applying Green's theorem?" :question/worked-solution "For a counterclockwise, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve $C$ bounding $D$, Green's theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,\\mathrm{d}x+Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nprovided $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$.\n\nThe region and its counterclockwise boundary are shown below.\n\n![](images/aa/aa60dd35a920d166f7cc3238a366bde93bd2c33e1b39b0a38313ab46a8de2d8b.png)\n\nHere $P(x,y)=y^2$ and $Q(x,y)=xy+x^2$. Holding $x$ constant in $P_y$ and $y$ constant in $Q_x$, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}&=2y,\\\\\n\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}&=y+2x.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x,y)&=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\\\\n&=(y+2x)-2y\\\\\n&=2x-y.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the line integral equals $\\iint_D(2x-y)\\,\\mathrm{d}A$. The task stops at the integrand; no integration is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701097 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of $f(x,y)=- 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ along the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 t^{2},- t^{2}\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le \\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{9}}.\n$$\n\nGive the exact value." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s &=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(3 t^{2})\\,i+(- t^{2})\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x=3 t^{2}$, $y=- t^{2}$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 t^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- t^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(6 t)\\,i+(- 2 t)\\,j \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(6 t)^2+(- 2 t)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40 t^{2}} \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{10}|t| \\\\  & =2 \\sqrt{10} t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|t|=t$ because $t\\geq0$ along the path of $C.$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=- 2 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =- 2 \\cos{\\left(3 t^{2} \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{9}}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\frac{\\pi}{9}}}(- 2 \\cos{\\left(3 t^{2} \\right)})(2 \\sqrt{10} t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=3 t^{2}$, $\\text{d}u=(6 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{10}}{3}\\cdot \\cos u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{10}}{3}\\sin u\\big|_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{10}}{3}[\\sin\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)-\\sin(0)] \\\\  & =- \\frac{2 \\sqrt{10}}{3}[\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)-(0)] \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{3}\\right)-(0) \\\\  & =- \\frac{\\sqrt{30}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701107 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of $f(x,y)=\\frac{x^{2}}{3} + \\frac{y^{2}}{3}$ along the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi}{6}.\n$$\n\nGive the exact value." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s &=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$, $y=- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j \\\\  & =(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(- 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(- 4 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9\\cos^2 t+16\\cos^2 t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}} \\\\  & =5|\\cos t| \\\\  & =5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $|\\cos t|=\\cos t$ because $\\cos t\\geq0$ along this interval.\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{x^{2}}{3} + \\frac{y^{2}}{3}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sin^2 t+16\\sin^2 t}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{25 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}\\left(\\frac{25 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{3}\\right)(5 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}\\frac{125 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}}{3}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$, $\\text{d}u=(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\frac{125}{3}\\cdot u^2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{125}{3}\\cdot\\frac{u^3}{3}\\big|_{0}^{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{125}{3}[\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^3}{3}-\\frac{(0)^3}{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{125}{3}[\\left(\\frac{1}{24}\\right)-(0)] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{125}{72}\\right)-(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{125}{72}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701129 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of $f(x,y,z)=6 y z \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ along the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle t,2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nGive the exact value." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s &=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(t)\\,i+(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j+(1)\\,k$, then along the curve we have $x=t$, $y=2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$, $z=1$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,k \\\\  & =(1)\\,i+(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j+(0)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1)^2+(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(0)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe substitution decreases from $5$ to $1$; its differential includes the negative sign.\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=6 y z \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(6)(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})(1)\\cos(t) \\\\  & =12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}(12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})(\\sqrt{4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 1})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=4 \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 1$, $\\text{d}u=(- 8 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={\\int }_{5}^{1}- \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =- \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{5}^{1} \\\\  & =-1[(1)^{3/2}-(5)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =-1[(1)-(5 \\sqrt{5})] \\\\  & =(-1)-(- 5 \\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =-1 + 5 \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701130 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ along the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2},t\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\n$$\n\nGive the exact value." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s &=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(2 t^{2})\\,i+(t)\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x=2 t^{2}$, $y=t$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =(4 t)\\,i+(1)\\,j \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4 t)^2+(1)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1(2 t^{2})}{(t)-(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t^2}{t} \\\\  & =2 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}^{\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}}(2 t)(\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 1})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=16 t^{2} + 1$, $\\text{d}u=(32 t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={\\int }_{9}^{25}\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{9}^{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}[(25)^{3/2}-(9)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}[(125)-(27)] \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{125}{24}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701131 :question/problem "Evaluate the line integral of $f(x,y)=x^{3} + 3 y$ along the curve\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3 t,t^{3}\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nGive the exact value." :question/worked-solution "To calculate the line integral, we will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s &=\\int_a^b f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r(t)=(3 t)\\,i+(t^{3})\\,j$, then along the curve we have $x=3 t$, $y=t^{3}$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =(3)\\,i+(3 t^{2})\\,j \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\| & =\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3)^2+(3 t^{2})^2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9 t^{4} + 9} \\\\  & =3 \\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x^{3} + 3 y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(3 t)^3+(3)(t^{3}) \\\\  & =27t^3+3t^3 \\\\  & =30 t^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & =\\int_C f\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(30 t^{3})(3 \\sqrt{t^{4} + 1})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}90 t^{3} \\sqrt{t^{4} + 1}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=t^{4} + 1$, $\\text{d}u=(4 t^{3})\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{45}{2}\\cdot u^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{45}{2}\\cdot\\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2}\\big|_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =15[(2)^{3/2}-(1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =15[(2 \\sqrt{2})-(1)] \\\\  & =(30 \\sqrt{2})-(15) \\\\  & =-15 + 30 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186701133 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s$, where $f(x,y,z)=- z \\left(x^{2} - y^{2}\\right)$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, 2 t\\rangle,\\qquad \\frac{\\pi}{6}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nDo not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate each coordinate and compute the speed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r'(t)&=\\langle - \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}, \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, 2\\rangle \\\\ \\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)})^2+(2)^2}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\nSubstitute the coordinates into the scalar function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf r(t))&=- 2 t \\left(- \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiply by speed and retain the parameter bounds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}f(\\mathbf r(t))\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}(- 2 t \\left(- \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right))(\\sqrt{5})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}}- 2 \\sqrt{5} t \\left(- \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
